{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NgeXDfqM9PtTSYbX2t9LnB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HheBx243QSsPDyncoDXSCF","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Review Report: Permutation-Calibrated Temporal DIF Reveals 7–17% Genuine Item Instability Across Six LLM Benchmarks\n\n**Venue:** EMNLP 2026 (ARR)\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nThis paper applies the Mantel-Haenszel (MH) Differential Item Functioning (DIF) procedure to LLM benchmark response matrices (primarily METABENCH MMLU, 5,227 models × 12,508 items) to detect items whose difficulty shifts between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts. The headline raw finding of 31.3% ETS-C items is then calibrated via a 1,000-iteration permutation test, revealing that ~40% of the raw signal is attributable to temporal cohort structure, reducing genuine instability to ~17% on MMLU and 7–17% across six benchmarks. A semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching is introduced (ΔFPR < 0.02), and logistic regression analysis uncovers a Simpson's paradox in DIF predictors.\n\n---\n\n## Soundness: 2.5 / 5\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Permutation calibration is the right instinct.** Recognizing that naive MH-DIF on temporal cohorts is inflated and providing a quantitative correction (null = 12.4%, z = 10.5) is methodologically responsible. The decomposition into release-date clustering (~7 pp), training-data overlap (~3 pp), and residual (~2.4 pp) adds interpretive value.\n\n2. **Semi-synthetic validation framework.** The ΔTPR/ΔFPR methodology with cross-subject matching is a genuine methodological insight. The demonstration that within-subject matching contaminates the conditioning variable (ΔFPR = 0.196 at p=1.0 vs. 0.018 for cross-subject) is a useful finding for future work.\n\n3. **Sanity checks are thorough.** Random-split controls (<1% across all benchmarks), ability-quintile DIF (C% = 2.9%), and architecture stratification (99.98% decoder-only) demonstrate careful experimental design.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **No methodological contribution.** MH-DIF (1959/1988) is applied without modification. Permutation testing is a standard calibration technique. The paper applies existing psychometric tools to a new domain — this is acknowledged (line 147–149: \"the contribution is empirical\") but for EMNLP the empirical contribution must then be correspondingly strong. It is not (see below).\n\n2. **The ability confound is insufficiently resolved.** The paper acknowledges that nearest-neighbor matching reduces C% by only 2.4 pp (lines 398–401) and that 6–12 pp of residual confounding may remain from unmatched latent dimensions (lines 401–404). This is a critical issue: the 57-dimensional multidimensionality of MMLU means that total-score stratification in MH-DIF is misspecified. The bifactor IRT pilot failed on 34/57 subjects with poor fit even on converged subjects (RMSEA = 0.089, CFI = 0.74). The defense that \"MH conditions on observed total score (nonparametric)\" is technically correct but does not address the fact that total score is a poor summary of a 57-dimensional ability space. If models differ on latent dimensions not captured by total score, MH will flag these differences as DIF when they are actually ability differences.\n\n3. **GSM1K negative correlation undermines the diagnostic interpretation.** r = −0.478 (p = 0.005) between DIF effects and GSM8K-vs-GSM1K gaps is consistent with DIF capturing ability improvement rather than item instability. The paper correctly flags this as exploratory (N = 33), but the CI excludes zero ([−0.71, −0.16]), making it difficult to dismiss. The paper does not provide a compelling alternative explanation.\n\n4. **Calibrated magnitudes are underwhelming.** After permutation calibration, HellaSwag shows 7.3% genuine instability and GSM8K 9.7%. These are modest numbers for a headline finding. The paper title promises \"7–17% genuine item instability\" — the lower end of this range is close to what might be expected from residual confounds that permutation calibration does not fully address.\n\n5. **Item count correlation with C% (r = 0.72) is concerning.** The paper acknowledges this (lines 252–254) and argues permutation calibration \"partially accounts\" for it, but the residual correlation after calibration (Spearman ρ = 0.886 preserved rank order) suggests a systematic relationship between benchmark size and detected DIF that may be artifactual. Larger item pools provide more opportunities for stochastic DIF flags, and the permutation null may not fully remove this.\n\n6. **ΔFPR is not zero.** The paper claims ΔFPR < 0.02 as a selling point, but this is a retreat from the \"provable FPR control\" that a zero ΔFPR would provide. 1.2–1.8% residual FPR inflation means the semi-synthetic framework does not perfectly separate injected signal from ambient DIF, reducing confidence in power estimates.\n\n---\n\n## Excitement: 2 / 5\n\n### What works\n\n- The research question is timely and important: measurement invariance of LLM benchmarks is under-examined.\n- The permutation calibration idea, while not novel statistically, is well-motivated for the LLM evaluation community and could become standard practice.\n\n### What doesn't\n\n1. **The \"diagnostic lens\" framing is post-hoc.** The paper's original motivation appears to be regeneration (replacing unstable items), but several experiments failed (bifactor IRT non-convergence, item generation ρ = 0.48, ensemble killed). The pivot to \"diagnostic lens\" is visible in the text but not fully convincing. The paper doesn't demonstrate what a practitioner should *do* with the knowledge that 7–17% of items are unstable.\n\n2. **Calibrated numbers are not dramatic enough for a long paper.** \"~17% of MMLU items show genuine temporal instability\" is interesting but not surprising given that 5,000+ models spanning multiple architecture generations and training paradigms are compared. The 7% HellaSwag number is hard to distinguish from noise/residual confounds.\n\n3. **Appendix experiments are uniformly negative or inconclusive.** RL robustness is underpowered (N=13). ConStat overlap is near-zero (Jaccard = 0.11). DVD is a near-null result. Ensemble was killed. Bifactor failed. Item generation quality was insufficient. Cumulative Bayesian DIF fell short of power targets. This pattern suggests the research program struggled to find strong results, and the main body cherry-picks the subset that survived.\n\n4. **Missing actionable recommendations.** The paper identifies instability but does not provide a clear protocol for benchmark maintainers. The graceful degradation analysis (§4.8) is a step in this direction but remains hypothetical (replacement items were not actually validated).\n\n5. **Limited novelty for NLP audience.** Applying MH-DIF to LLM benchmarks is a domain transfer, not a methodological advance. The permutation calibration is standard. The semi-synthetic framework is useful but narrow. For EMNLP, the contribution needs to either (a) introduce new methods or (b) produce findings that change how the community builds/uses benchmarks. The findings here suggest ~17% instability on MMLU after calibration — interesting but not actionable without further work.\n\n---\n\n## Reproducibility: 3.5 / 5\n\n- METABENCH is publicly available, and the MH-DIF procedure is standard.\n- Permutation calibration with 1,000 iterations is straightforward to replicate.\n- Semi-synthetic injection methodology is clearly described.\n- However, several specifics are missing: exact model lists for reference/focal cohorts, pre-2024 vs. 2024+ cutoff date, how ties in release date are handled, which 150/500 items were selected for semi-synthetic injection.\n- Code and data are not mentioned as being released (no reproducibility statement visible in the paper).\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Comments\n\n### Major Issues\n\n**M1. The multidimensionality problem is more serious than acknowledged.** MH-DIF conditions on total score, but MMLU has 57 subjects with heterogeneous difficulty and content. Two models with the same total score may have very different subject-level profiles. When temporal cohorts differ in subject-level ability profiles (which they do — domain ρ = 0.36 with accuracy Δ), MH will interpret this as item-level DIF when it is actually multidimensional ability difference. The paper's own IRT diagnostics (CFI = 0.71, TLI = 0.68) confirm massive misfit. The Limitations section mentions this but the main text proceeds as if it is not a problem.\n\n**M2. The permutation null may not be the correct null.** The permutation destroys temporal ordering while preserving group sizes, but temporal cohort structure (release-date clustering, training-data overlap) is *part of what makes temporal DIF interesting*. If the question is \"do items function differently for 2023 vs. 2024 models?\", then the cohort structure is the signal, not noise. The paper defines \"genuine\" instability as the excess over permutation null, but an equally valid framing is that the full 31.3% represents how items actually behave differently across these groups. The choice of null is a framing decision, not a methodological one, and the paper presents it as objective.\n\n**M3. GSM1K finding needs more than \"exploratory\" dismissal.** The negative correlation (r = −0.478, p = 0.005, CI excluding zero) between DIF effects and contamination-free performance gaps is the most threatening finding to the paper's narrative. If DIF is primarily detecting ability improvement, the entire \"instability\" framing is misleading. The paper correctly notes N = 33 is small, but the effect is significant with a CI that excludes zero. This needs either a convincing mechanistic rebuttal or prominent repositioning of the paper's claims.\n\n**M4. Semi-synthetic ΔTPR is low at moderate contamination.** At p = 0.5 (half of focal models contaminated), ΔTPR = 0.254. This means DIF only detects a quarter of injected items beyond the ambient noise floor. For a method positioned as a \"diagnostic tool,\" this detection power is insufficient for practical deployment. The paper somewhat acknowledges this (lines 365–369) but still lists the framework as Contribution 2.\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n**m1.** Table 1: The random split row lacks n_ref and n_foc values. How were groups sized?\n\n**m2.** The IFT analysis (§4.7) reveals 47% of flagged items show gradual drift rather than abrupt shifts, which undermines the binary temporal split used throughout the paper. This finding deserves more discussion than one paragraph.\n\n**m3.** References use \"and 1 others\" repeatedly (lines 533, 540, 543, 546, 561) — this appears to be a formatting error or placeholder. Several references are incomplete (\"arXiv preprint\" without IDs).\n\n**m4.** The paper does not discuss the Responsible NLP Research Checklist, which is mandatory for ARR submissions.\n\n**m5.** Line 399 references \"plan_027\" — this appears to be an internal experiment label that leaked into the paper text.\n\n**m6.** The graceful degradation analysis (§4.8) uses \"LLM-generated alternatives\" but the item generation calibration (Appendix F) shows only 41% quality pass rate with ρ = 0.48. The degradation analysis assumes replacement items are valid, which Appendix F shows is not the case.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. How do you respond to the interpretation that DIF is primarily detecting multidimensional ability improvement (supported by GSM1K r = −0.478 and domain ρ = 0.36) rather than item instability?\n\n2. Why is the permutation null the appropriate null hypothesis? What would change if you used a parametric null that models temporal cohort structure explicitly?\n\n3. Can you provide subject-level DIF results (e.g., C% per MMLU subject after permutation calibration) to test whether instability is concentrated in specific domains?\n\n4. The IFT finding that 47% of DIF items show gradual drift — does this not invalidate the binary temporal split used for all other analyses?\n\n---\n\n## Missing Experiments (vs. Design Document)\n\nThe review context lists several experiments that appear in the paper only as appendix entries or are absent:\n\n- **Bifactor IRT equating (plan_005):** Failed, reported in Appendix E. Appropriately handled.\n- **Item generation calibration (plan_006):** Reported in Appendix F with poor results. Not discussed in main text.\n- **Cumulative Bayesian DIF (plan_007):** Reported in Appendix G. Did not reach power target. Appropriately scoped.\n- **RL robustness (plan_002):** Appendix A, honestly described as underpowered. Good.\n- **ConStat (plan_003a), DVD (plan_003b), Ensemble (plan_003c):** Appendices B–D. All negative/null results. Honest reporting.\n\nThe design document's narrative guidance accurately predicted reviewer concerns: inflated headline, DIF-as-ability-proxy, no methodological contribution. The paper's attempt to reframe around permutation calibration methodology is partially successful but the calibrated numbers (7–17%) are not dramatic enough to carry a long paper.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment: 2.5 (Borderline Findings)\n\nThe paper addresses an important question (measurement invariance of LLM benchmarks) with careful empirical work and commendable honesty about negative results. The permutation calibration insight is useful and the semi-synthetic framework has reuse value. However, the lack of methodological novelty (MH-DIF applied as-is), the unresolved ability confound (multidimensionality, GSM1K negative correlation), the underwhelming calibrated magnitudes (7–17%), and the absence of actionable recommendations limit the contribution below the EMNLP Main conference threshold. The paper would benefit from (1) a multidimensional DIF method that addresses the 57-subject structure, (2) a more convincing resolution of the ability-vs-instability confound, and (3) a concrete audit protocol for benchmark maintainers. In its current form, the paper is a solid empirical report that would be appropriate for Findings after addressing the major issues, particularly M1 (multidimensionality) and M3 (GSM1K interpretation).\n\n**Soundness:** 2.5 / 5\n**Excitement:** 2 / 5\n**Reproducibility:** 3.5 / 5\n**Overall Assessment:** 2.5 — Borderline Findings\n\n**Confidence:** 4 / 5 — I am confident in this assessment. The psychometric methodology is well-established and I can evaluate its application. The statistical analyses are straightforward. My main uncertainty is whether the NLP community would find the permutation calibration insight sufficiently novel to offset the other weaknesses.","project":"irt_equated_bench_regen","tier":"worst","dream_id":"20260521T122440","overall_score":"2.5 — Borderline Findings","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":845,"cache_read_input_tokens":63255,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":845},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"59e9cceb-2107-44b0-875b-8adf30456f69","uuid":"2b4aa59d-310e-4bbd-a595-062f3fb60842"}
